Term premium is always “incredibly un-useful”, unless you are trying to make a decomposition or attribution of the sources of change to multi-year nominal bond yields into expected short-term rates and risk compensation, which is already a dangerous exercise. 

Some go further and want to transform the decomposition into real growth expectations and inflation expectations while thinking they’ve sidestepped the measurement issues with short rate expectations. 

Ultimately, it comes down to having a theoretical point of view or judgment, which is often dangerously close to being useless hence not even wrong.

